Practical 3: Linear Regression Using CSV Data
Aim: Implement Linear Regression using a training dataset stored in a CSV file and calculate Mean Squared Error (MSE) on test data.

Dataset: Real Diabetes dataset available in Scikit-learn. BMI is used as the input feature, and disease-progression measure is the target.
Complete Python program




import pandas as pd
from sklearn.datasets import load_diabetes
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error

# Load the real Diabetes dataset
diabetes = load_diabetes(as_frame=True)

data = diabetes.frame[["bmi", "target"]].rename(
    columns={"bmi": "X", "target": "Y"}
)

# Split the dataset
train, test = train_test_split(
    data, test_size=0.25, random_state=42
)

# Save the training and testing data as CSV files
train.to_csv("linear_train.csv", index=False)
test.to_csv("linear_test.csv", index=False)

# Read the CSV files
train_data = pd.read_csv("linear_train.csv")
test_data = pd.read_csv("linear_test.csv")

# Separate features and target
X_train = train_data[["X"]]
y_train = train_data["Y"]

X_test = test_data[["X"]]
y_test = test_data["Y"]

# Create and train the Linear Regression model
model = LinearRegression()
model.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Predict target values
predictions = model.predict(X_test)

# Calculate Mean Squared Error
mse = mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions)

print("Training Samples:", len(X_train))
print("Testing Samples:", len(X_test))
print("Predictions:", predictions[:10])
print("Actual Values:", y_test.to_numpy()[:10])
print("Mean Squared Error:", mse)


Result: Linear Regression was implemented using CSV files generated from the real Diabetes dataset, and its predictive performance was evaluated using MSE.
